<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahmad Fadl al-Qumandan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1884–1943</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Ahmad Fadl al-Abdali</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ahmad Fadl al-Qumandan was a prince of the Abdali ruling house of the Sultanate of Lahej in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, a poet and musician who also commanded the sultanate's forces, from which he took the name al-Qumandan. His songs in the local dialect, set to Lahji melodies, founded a distinctive school of Lahji music that remains central to the musical heritage of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>.</p></div>
